 **//*
	 * These strings are in use by customers and should not be changed.
	 * The platform ID must appear at the end of the string.
	 *//* Last string in the hardware desc is the ID *//*
	 * BB should be active and out of reset. ok to query version register
	 *
	 * check that soft reset is low, and global enable is high
	 *//* A retained publication must never be overwritten by a retry. *//* Guard againt second call to function *//* Allocate what we are going to publish.
	 * Pointer can be used by any processor in system,
	 * so published pointer must be "bus" pointer.
	 * If other processors want to convert pointer for example
	 * to be non-cacheable, they must remap it themself.
	 * Structure must be allocated using dma_alloc_coherent().
	 *//* Initialize shared soc_shared_params structure *//* Initialize beamforming message box structure *//* Initialize RIFS mode structure *//* deferred channel switch *//* cca scan *//* SCS info *//* remain channel info *//* ocac *//* Measurement info *//* remain channel info *//* semaphore calltrace log *//* FIXME: to be replaced *//* bmps info *//* Fill shared parameters structure *//* This error is relatively harmless, so carry on. *//* Initialise flag for TQE hang WAR *//* This error is relatively harmless, so carry on. *//* This slow ethernet check is done twice in qdrv as sc is initialized at this point *//*
	 * CPUs will access the shared parameters.
	 *//* for RC only: if not PCIE_TQE_INTR_WORKAROUND ignore the dsp fw download *//* Bring up the DSP *//* Get the private device data *//* MATS FIX This should be initialized better somewhere else *//* Make sure we have firmware image specified for the MuC *//* Set the device in the control block *//* initiate the power control block *//*
	 * Reset the SoC.
	 *
	 * Must be called *before* qdrv_soc_publish_params, so that BB is out of reset
	 * when reading the version register
	 *//* Publish SoC parameters *//* Register scheduler *//* Initialize the MAC 0 device *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* Ruby (ARC host) does not support MAC 1 *//* Initialize the MAC 1 device *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* Initialize the message module *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* Bring up the DSP *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* Initialise MuC print buf *//* Bring up the MuC  *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* Bring up the AuC  *//* Mark that we have successfully allocated a resource *//* That went well .... *//* Unknown partial hardware ownership: retain allocations and callbacks. *//* Not so good .... *//* Get the private device data *//* Release resources in reverse order *//* Release resources in reverse order *//**
