 *//*
 * IEEE 802.11i AES-CCMP crypto support.
 *
 * Part of this module is derived from similar code in the Host
 * AP driver. The code is used with the consent of the author and
 * it's license is included below.
 *//* for diagnostics + statistics *//* Linux73 AES block library; CCMP only uses the forward AES primitive. *//*
 * Add privacy headers appropriate for the specified key.
 *//*
	 * Copy down 802.11 header and add the IV, KeyID, and ExtIV.
	 *//* XXX wrap at 48 bits *//* PN0 *//* PN1 *//* Reserved *//* KeyID | ExtID *//* PN2 *//* PN3 *//* PN4 *//* PN5 *//*
	 * Finally, do software encrypt if neeed.
	 *//*
 * Add MIC to the frame as needed.
 *//*
 * Validate and strip privacy headers (and trailer) for a
 * received frame. The specified key should be correct but
 * is also verified.
 *//*
	 * Header should have extended IV and sequence number;
	 * verify the former and validate the latter.
	 *//*
		 * No extended IV; discard frame.
		 *//* NB: assume IEEE80211_WEP_MINLEN covers the extended IV *//*
		 * Replay violation.
		 *//*
	 * Check if the device handled the decrypt in hardware.
	 * If so we just strip the header; otherwise we need to
	 * handle the decrypt in software.  Note that for the
	 * latter we leave the header in place for use in the
	 * decryption work.
	 *//*
	 * Copy up 802.11 header and strip crypto bits.
	 *//*
	 * Ok to update rsc now.
	 *//*
 * Verify and strip MIC from the frame.
 *//*
 * Host AP crypt: host-based CCMP encryption implementation for Host AP driver
 *
 * Copyright (c) 2003-2004, Jouni Malinen <jkmaline@cc.hut.fi>
 *
 * This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify
 * it under the terms of the GNU General Public License version 2 as
 * published by the Free Software Foundation. See README and COPYING for
 * more details.
 *
 * Alternatively, this software may be distributed under the terms of BSD
 * license.
 *//* CCM Initial Block:
	 * Flag (Include authentication header, M=3 (8-octet MIC),
	 *       L=1 (2-octet Dlen))
	 * Nonce: 0x00 | A2 | PN
	 * Dlen *//* NB: b0[1] set below *//* AAD:
	 * FC with bits 4..6 and 11..13 masked to zero; 14 is always one
	 * A1 | A2 | A3
	 * SC with bits 4..15 (seq#) masked to zero
	 * A4 (if present)
	 * QC (if present)
	 *//* AAD length >> 8 *//* NB: aad[1] set below *//* XXX magic #s *//* XXX magic #s *//* NB: we know 3 addresses are contiguous *//* all bits masked *//*
	 * Construct variable-length portion of AAD based
	 * on whether this is a 4-address frame/QOS frame.
	 * We always zero-pad to 32 bytes before running it
	 * through the cipher.
	 *
	 * We also fill in the priority bits of the CCM
	 * initial block as we know whether or not we have
	 * a QOS frame.
	 *//* just priority bits *//* just priority bits *//* Start with the first block and AAD *//* Authentication *//* Encryption, with counter *//* NB: should not happen *//* XXX statistic *//* NB: assumes header is entirely in first skbuf *//*
		 * Do full blocks.
		 *//* no more data *//* last buffer *//*
				 * Short last block.
				 *//*
			 * Block straddles buffers, split references.  We
			 * do not handle splits that require >2 buffers.
			 *//* XXX could check for data_len <= 0 *//*
			 * Setup for next buffer.
			 *//* tack on MIC *//* Decrypt, with counter *//* Authentication *//* NB: skb left pointing at last in chain *//* NB: this is the last in the chain *//* XXX assert skb->len >= ccmp.ic_trailer *//* no more data *//* last buffer *//* short last block *//*
			 * Block straddles buffers, split references.  We
			 * do not handle splits that require >2 buffers.
			 *//*
			 * Setup for next buffer.
			 *//*
 * Module glue.
 *//*-
